<h2 id="ras">A Near-Perfect Relative Athletic Score</h2>
<p>Williams's combine performance translated to a Relative Athletic Score (RAS) of 9.84 out of a possible 10.0 — a composite metric that averages the percentile ranking of every test a player completes relative to others at the same position. For context, a RAS above 9.0 is considered elite; anything above 9.5 is exceedingly rare for a player at 300-plus pounds. That number is a big part of why Williams was viewed as a near-consensus top-three prospect in a loaded 2019 defensive line class that also included Josh Allen, Rashan Gary, and Montez Sweat.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>What RAS Actually Measures</strong>
  Relative Athletic Score converts a player's raw combine numbers into percentiles based on position, then averages them into a single 0-to-10 score. It's designed to answer one specific question: how athletically special is this player relative to everyone else who has played their position? For the full breakdown of how it's calculated, see our <a class="inline" href="https://athletecalculator.com/relative-athletic-score-nfl">Relative Athletic Score explainer</a>.
</div>
<h2 id="production">On-Field Production</h2>
<p>Williams's athletic testing has held up on the field for seven NFL seasons. He made four straight Pro Bowls with the Jets and earned First Team All-Pro honors in 2022, and in just seven games with Dallas after last year's trade, he posted 21 tackles, three tackles for loss, 1.5 sacks, and an interception. That first-step quickness scouts flagged coming out of Alabama — powered by the same explosiveness that showed up in his jump testing — has remained his signature trait at the NFL level.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is a good 40-yard dash time for a defensive tackle?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Most defensive tackles run between 4.9 and 5.3 seconds at the combine. A time under 4.9 seconds, like Quinnen Williams's 4.83, is considered exceptional for the position, especially at over 300 pounds.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What is a Relative Athletic Score (RAS)?</div>
<div class="faq-a">RAS is a composite score from 0 to 10 that converts a player's combine testing results into position-specific percentiles, then averages them into a single number. It's designed to measure overall athleticism relative to other players at the same position across NFL history.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Why did the Jets trade Quinnen Williams if he was this good?</div>
<div class="faq-a">The Jets were in the midst of a rebuild after a 1-7 start to the 2025 season and needed draft capital. Trading Williams brought back a 2027 first-round pick and a 2026 second-round pick, allowing New York to build out a younger, more cost-effective defensive line rotation.</div>
</div>
